initial commit:

This version upon start checks if there is a movie cache and if not it pulls all 500 pages * 20 movies of the most popular from tmdb to build a database of 10000 movies
clicking on the moviecard or a torrent ijn the list starts a download, movie automatically plays when there is data buffered

known issue:

the when clicking on the next/prev page buttons the total pages count changes and its slow
I think the search is being run every time

fixed - pagination now uses in-memory data:

- Added /api/popular/all endpoint to return full movie list from cache
- Frontend now loads all popular movies once on startup
- Next/Prev buttons now slice from in-memory array instead of calling API
- Page count is calculated from array length, no longer fluctuates
- Search results also stored in-memory for pagination
- Initial search still shows live progress, but navigation after is instant

changes:

- reorganized cache storage:
  - Created database/ directory for movie cache
  - Renamed movies_cache.json to database/active.json
  - Updated CACHE_FILE path in movietime

- added dark scrollbars to match the theme

- removed 24 hour cache expiry, cache now never expires

- added continuous background movie sync:
  - On app start, background thread continuously fetches TMDB pages one at a time
  - New movies are added incrementally to avoid duplicates (O(1) lookup via set)
  - Saves sync state (current_page) to database/sync_state.json for resume on restart
  - After page 500, loops back to page 1
  - Rate limited to 25 req/sec (0.04 sec between requests)
  - Uses atomic file writes (write to temp, then rename) for safe shutdown
  - Graceful shutdown: sets stop event on exit, waits up to 3 seconds for thread to finish
  - Added /api/sync/status endpoint to monitor sync progress
  - Removed initial bulk fetch - now handled by continuous background sync
  - Updated /api/cache/refresh to indicate continuous sync is running
  - Writes to disk every 100 pages to reduce I/O overhead

- replaced single-file approach with dual-file append-only system:
  - active.json: main movie database (line-delimited JSON)
  - building.json: append-only file for new movies (line-delimited JSON)
  - On each page: append new movies to building.json, validate, then atomic swap
  - After swap: building contains old active data + new movies (both in sync)
  - Only writes ~20 new movies per page instead of rewriting entire dataset
  - O(1) I/O regardless of total database size
  - Added recovery: if one file corrupted, copy from the other
  - Both files now use consistent line-delimited format
  - Added ensure_database_files() to handle first-run edge cases
  - Fixed validation to properly parse line-delimited JSON
  - Fixed load_cached_movies() to use line-delimited format parser

- added genre-based discovery:
  - Uses /discover/movie with with_genres parameter to fetch by genre
  - 19 genre IDs defined (Action, Adventure, Animation, Comedy, etc.)
  - Two-phase sync: Phase 1 = popular (500 pages), Phase 2 = interleaved genre search
  - Interleaved: For each page 1-500, cycles through all 19 genres
  - Sync state now tracks current_page, genre_index, and phase
  - Saves state after each page for resume capability
  - Reuses Update Movies button as manual refresh to reload movie list from database
  - Renamed /api/popular/all to /api/movies/all to reflect it now returns all movies (popular + genre)

- replaced Update button with automatic movie count display:
   - Removed Update Movies button entirely
   - Added movie count display (e.g., "4,523 Movies") in pagination area
   - Auto-refresh polls every 10 seconds to check for new movies
   - Count updates automatically without user interaction
   - Layout: sort | n Movies | prev | page n of n | next

- added genre-based filtering:
   - Replaced Type dropdown with Genre dropdown (19 genres)
   - Frontend filters movies by genre_ids field
   - Backend now includes genre_ids in movie data

- added "N of T Movies" display:
   - Shows filtered count (N) of total cache count (T)
   - When search or genre filter active, displays "N of T Movies"
   - When no filter, displays just "T Movies"

- fixed auto-refresh movie count bug:
   - Auto-refresh now only updates movie cache, not display
   - Previously auto-refresh tried to recalculate N and showed wrong count
   - Display "N of T" stays until user performs new search
